[{"location":"generated/tutorials/gettingstarted/","page":"-","title":"-","text":"EditURL = \"../../../literate/tutorials/gettingstarted.jl\"","category":"page"},{"location":"generated/tutorials/gettingstarted/","page":"-","title":"-","text":"Autoencoder                  Team:Shaolong, Ramnath Rao Bekal, Rahul Bhaskaram                                                       31/8/2024","category":"page"},{"location":"generated/tutorials/gettingstarted/","page":"-","title":"-","text":"1.Definition 1.Learning rate Too high a learning rate might cause the model to oscillate or even diverge during training, leading to poor convergence. This could result in poor quality of the graphics generated by the autoencoder, with significant loss of detail. Too low a learning rate might lead to excessively slow training and the model might get stuck in local minima. This could cause the autoencoder to generate overly smooth images that miss some important features.","category":"page"},{"location":"generated/tutorials/gettingstarted/","page":"-","title":"-","text":"Epoch Number","category":"page"},{"location":"generated/tutorials/gettingstarted/","page":"-","title":"-","text":"Too few epochs might prevent the model from adequately learning the features in the data, affecting the quality and accuracy of the reconstructed graphics. Too many epochs might lead to overfitting where the model performs well on the training data but poorly on new, unseen data. An overfitted autoencoder might generate images that are too reliant on specific noise and details of the training data, instead of learning a more general representation of the data.","category":"page"},{"location":"generated/tutorials/gettingstarted/","page":"-","title":"-","text":"Batch Size","category":"page"},{"location":"generated/tutorials/gettingstarted/","page":"-","title":"-","text":"Smaller batch sizes generally provide a more accurate gradient estimation but might lead to a less stable training process and longer training times. Smaller batches might enable the model to learn more details, potentially leading to better performance in image reconstruction tasks. Larger batch sizes can speed up training and stabilize gradient estimations but may reduce the generalization ability of the model during training. In autoencoders, too large a batch might result in reconstructed images that lack detail and appear more blurred or smooth.","category":"page"},{"location":"generated/tutorials/gettingstarted/","page":"-","title":"-","text":"Mean Squared Error (MSE)","category":"page"},{"location":"generated/tutorials/gettingstarted/","page":"-","title":"-","text":"a commonly used metric to measure the average squared difference between the observed actual values (targets) and the values predicted by a model. The MSE loss rate is calculated as the mean of the squares of these differences. It quantifies how well the model's predictions match the actual data, with a lower MSE indicating better performance. 5.R-Squared error(R²) also known as the coefficient of determination, is a statistical measure that indicates how well the variance in the dependent variable is explained by the independent variables in the model. It ranges from 0 to 1, with values closer to 1 indicating a better fit. R² loss rate is often used to describe the proportion of the variance that is not explained by the model.","category":"page"},{"location":"generated/tutorials/gettingstarted/","page":"-","title":"-","text":"2.Code explanation","category":"page"},{"location":"generated/tutorials/gettingstarted/","page":"-","title":"-","text":"First Formula: Includes the effects of both sight and hearing","category":"page"},{"location":"generated/tutorials/gettingstarted/","page":"-","title":"-","text":"f = @formula 0 ~ 0 + sight + hearing 2.Second Formula: Includes only the effect of hearing fhearing = @formula 0 ~ 0 + hearing 3.Third Formula: Includes only the effect of sight fsight = @formula 0 ~ 0 + sight","category":"page"},{"location":"generated/tutorials/gettingstarted/","page":"-","title":"-","text":"","category":"page"},{"location":"generated/tutorials/gettingstarted/","page":"-","title":"-","text":"This page was generated using Literate.jl.","category":"page"},{"location":"generated/tutorials/test/","page":"-","title":"-","text":"EditURL = \"../../../literate/tutorials/test.jl\"","category":"page"},{"location":"generated/tutorials/test/","page":"-","title":"-","text":"","category":"page"},{"location":"generated/tutorials/test/","page":"-","title":"-","text":"This page was generated using Literate.jl.","category":"page"},{"location":"generated/tutorials/Autoencoder_EEG_Meeting_Minutes/","page":"Autoencoder","title":"Autoencoder","text":"EditURL = \"../../../literate/tutorials/Autoencoder_EEG_Meeting_Minutes.jl\"","category":"page"},{"location":"generated/tutorials/Autoencoder_EEG_Meeting_Minutes/#Autoencoder","page":"Autoencoder","title":"Autoencoder","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"generated/tutorials/Autoencoder_EEG_Meeting_Minutes/#Team:-Shaolong,-Ramnath-Rao-Bekal,-Rahul-Bhaskaram","page":"Autoencoder","title":"Team: Shaolong, Ramnath Rao Bekal, Rahul Bhaskaram","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"generated/tutorials/Autoencoder_EEG_Meeting_Minutes/#Date:-31/8/2024","page":"Autoencoder","title":"Date: 31/8/2024","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"generated/tutorials/Autoencoder_EEG_Meeting_Minutes/#1.-Definition","page":"Autoencoder","title":"1. Definition","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"generated/tutorials/Autoencoder_EEG_Meeting_Minutes/#What-is-Autoencoder?","page":"Autoencoder","title":"What is Autoencoder?","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"generated/tutorials/Autoencoder_EEG_Meeting_Minutes/","page":"Autoencoder","title":"Autoencoder","text":"An autoencoder is a type of artificial neural network used to learn efficient representations of data, typically for the purpose of dimensionality reduction or feature learning. It consists of two main parts:","category":"page"},{"location":"generated/tutorials/Autoencoder_EEG_Meeting_Minutes/","page":"Autoencoder","title":"Autoencoder","text":"Encoder: This part compresses the input data into a lower-dimensional representation,   capturing the essential features while discarding noise and redundancy.   The encoder typically reduces the input dimensions to a much smaller size.\nDecoder: This part reconstructs the original input from the compressed representation.   The goal of the decoder is to produce an output as close as possible to the original input data.","category":"page"},{"location":"generated/tutorials/Autoencoder_EEG_Meeting_Minutes/","page":"Autoencoder","title":"Autoencoder","text":"Autoencoders are trained using unsupervised learning, where the network learns to minimize the difference between the input and the output (reconstruction error). They are widely used in applications such as data compression, denoising, anomaly detection, and feature extraction.","category":"page"},{"location":"generated/tutorials/Autoencoder_EEG_Meeting_Minutes/#A-List-of-Examples","page":"Autoencoder","title":"A List of Examples","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"generated/tutorials/Autoencoder_EEG_Meeting_Minutes/#1.-Medical-Imaging","page":"Autoencoder","title":"1. Medical Imaging","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"generated/tutorials/Autoencoder_EEG_Meeting_Minutes/","page":"Autoencoder","title":"Autoencoder","text":"Scenario: A doctor needs to analyze a large set of medical images, such as MRIs, to detect any abnormalities.\nApplication: Autoencoders can help enhance the image quality or highlight areas of concern, making it easier for doctors to identify potential health issues, such as tumors or fractures.","category":"page"},{"location":"generated/tutorials/Autoencoder_EEG_Meeting_Minutes/#2.-Face-Recognition-on-Social-Media","page":"Autoencoder","title":"2. Face Recognition on Social Media","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"generated/tutorials/Autoencoder_EEG_Meeting_Minutes/","page":"Autoencoder","title":"Autoencoder","text":"Scenario: Social media platforms automatically tag people in photos.\nApplication: Autoencoders can compress and extract essential features from images to recognize faces, making it easier to tag friends in photos without manual input.","category":"page"},{"location":"generated/tutorials/Autoencoder_EEG_Meeting_Minutes/#3.-Photo-and-Video-Compression","page":"Autoencoder","title":"3. Photo and Video Compression","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"generated/tutorials/Autoencoder_EEG_Meeting_Minutes/","page":"Autoencoder","title":"Autoencoder","text":"Scenario: When you want to save space on your smartphone or computer by compressing photos or videos.\nApplication: An autoencoder can reduce the file size of images or videos while preserving important details, allowing you to store more files without significantly losing quality.","category":"page"},{"location":"generated/tutorials/Autoencoder_EEG_Meeting_Minutes/#4.-Noise-Reduction-in-Audio","page":"Autoencoder","title":"4. Noise Reduction in Audio","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"generated/tutorials/Autoencoder_EEG_Meeting_Minutes/","page":"Autoencoder","title":"Autoencoder","text":"Scenario: You have a recording from a crowded place, like a lecture or meeting, with a lot of background noise.\nApplication: A denoising autoencoder can clean up the audio by filtering out the background noise, making the speech clearer and easier to understand.","category":"page"},{"location":"generated/tutorials/Autoencoder_EEG_Meeting_Minutes/#5.-Detecting-Fraudulent-Transactions","page":"Autoencoder","title":"5. Detecting Fraudulent Transactions","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"generated/tutorials/Autoencoder_EEG_Meeting_Minutes/","page":"Autoencoder","title":"Autoencoder","text":"Scenario: Banks want to monitor transactions for potential fraud, such as unusual spending patterns on a credit card.\nApplication: An autoencoder trained on normal transaction data can identify transactions that significantly deviate from typical behavior, flagging them as potentially fraudulent.","category":"page"},{"location":"generated/tutorials/Autoencoder_EEG_Meeting_Minutes/#Key-Concept","page":"Autoencoder","title":"Key Concept","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"generated/tutorials/Autoencoder_EEG_Meeting_Minutes/","page":"Autoencoder","title":"Autoencoder","text":"Autoencoders are a versatile tool in machine learning for tasks such as dimensionality reduction, anomaly detection, and data compression. They work by learning to encode the input data into a lower-dimensional representation and then decode it back to its original form.","category":"page"},{"location":"generated/tutorials/Autoencoder_EEG_Meeting_Minutes/","page":"Autoencoder","title":"Autoencoder","text":"1.1 Learning Rate","category":"page"},{"location":"generated/tutorials/Autoencoder_EEG_Meeting_Minutes/","page":"Autoencoder","title":"Autoencoder","text":"Too high a learning rate might cause the model to oscillate or even diverge during training, leading to poor convergence. This could result in poor quality of the graphics generated by the autoencoder, with significant loss of detail.\nToo low a learning rate might lead to excessively slow training and the model might get stuck in local minima. This could cause the autoencoder to generate overly smooth images that miss some important features.","category":"page"},{"location":"generated/tutorials/Autoencoder_EEG_Meeting_Minutes/","page":"Autoencoder","title":"Autoencoder","text":"1.2 Epoch Number","category":"page"},{"location":"generated/tutorials/Autoencoder_EEG_Meeting_Minutes/","page":"Autoencoder","title":"Autoencoder","text":"Too few epochs might prevent the model from adequately learning the features in the data, affecting the quality and accuracy of the reconstructed graphics.\nToo many epochs might lead to overfitting where the model performs well on the training data but poorly on new, unseen data. An overfitted autoencoder might generate images that are too reliant on specific noise and details of the training data, instead of learning a more general representation of the data.","category":"page"},{"location":"generated/tutorials/Autoencoder_EEG_Meeting_Minutes/","page":"Autoencoder","title":"Autoencoder","text":"1.3 Batch Size","category":"page"},{"location":"generated/tutorials/Autoencoder_EEG_Meeting_Minutes/","page":"Autoencoder","title":"Autoencoder","text":"Smaller batch sizes generally provide a more accurate gradient estimation but might lead to a less stable training process and longer training times. Smaller batches might enable the model to learn more details, potentially leading to better performance in image reconstruction tasks.\nLarger batch sizes can speed up training and stabilize gradient estimations but may reduce the generalization ability of the model during training. In autoencoders, too large a batch might result in reconstructed images that lack detail and appear more blurred or smooth.","category":"page"},{"location":"generated/tutorials/Autoencoder_EEG_Meeting_Minutes/","page":"Autoencoder","title":"Autoencoder","text":"1.4 Mean Squared Error (MSE)","category":"page"},{"location":"generated/tutorials/Autoencoder_EEG_Meeting_Minutes/","page":"Autoencoder","title":"Autoencoder","text":"MSE is a commonly used metric to measure the average squared difference between the observed actual values (targets) and the values predicted by a model. The MSE loss rate is calculated as the mean of the squares of these differences. It quantifies how well the model's predictions match the actual data, with a lower MSE indicating better performance.","category":"page"},{"location":"generated/tutorials/Autoencoder_EEG_Meeting_Minutes/","page":"Autoencoder","title":"Autoencoder","text":"1.5 R-Squared Error (R²)","category":"page"},{"location":"generated/tutorials/Autoencoder_EEG_Meeting_Minutes/","page":"Autoencoder","title":"Autoencoder","text":"R², also known as the coefficient of determination, is a statistical measure that indicates how well the variance in the dependent variable is explained by the independent variables in the model. It ranges from 0 to 1, with values closer to 1 indicating a better fit. R² loss rate is often used to describe the proportion of the variance that is not explained by the model.","category":"page"},{"location":"generated/tutorials/Autoencoder_EEG_Meeting_Minutes/#2.-Code-Explanation","page":"Autoencoder","title":"2. Code Explanation","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"generated/tutorials/Autoencoder_EEG_Meeting_Minutes/","page":"Autoencoder","title":"Autoencoder","text":"1. First Formula: Includes the effects of both sight and hearing","category":"page"},{"location":"generated/tutorials/Autoencoder_EEG_Meeting_Minutes/","page":"Autoencoder","title":"Autoencoder","text":"f = @formula 0 ~ 0 + sight + hearing","category":"page"},{"location":"generated/tutorials/Autoencoder_EEG_Meeting_Minutes/","page":"Autoencoder","title":"Autoencoder","text":"2. Second Formula: Includes only the effect of hearing","category":"page"},{"location":"generated/tutorials/Autoencoder_EEG_Meeting_Minutes/","page":"Autoencoder","title":"Autoencoder","text":"f_hearing = @formula 0 ~ 0 + hearing","category":"page"},{"location":"generated/tutorials/Autoencoder_EEG_Meeting_Minutes/","page":"Autoencoder","title":"Autoencoder","text":"3. Third Formula: Includes only the effect of sight","category":"page"},{"location":"generated/tutorials/Autoencoder_EEG_Meeting_Minutes/","page":"Autoencoder","title":"Autoencoder","text":"f_sight = @formula 0 ~ 0 + sight","category":"page"},{"location":"generated/tutorials/Autoencoder_EEG_Meeting_Minutes/","page":"Autoencoder","title":"Autoencoder","text":"╔═╡ 549d0f75-be94-4460-9085-022f35613b29\nf = @formula 0 ~ 0 + sight + hearing\n\n╔═╡ 9ce43061-34bb-4905-b7e2-8fc5f96222cb\nf_hearing = @formula 0 ~ 0 + hearing\n\n╔═╡ e28911d6-3313-4504-90af-0cccae9d0dd8\nf_sight = @formula 0 ~ 0 + sight","category":"page"},{"location":"generated/tutorials/Autoencoder_EEG_Meeting_Minutes/","page":"Autoencoder","title":"Autoencoder","text":"To test and train the Deep Recurrent Encoder model, the following parameters are required:","category":"page"},{"location":"generated/tutorials/Autoencoder_EEG_Meeting_Minutes/","page":"Autoencoder","title":"Autoencoder","text":"Parameter 1: Random number generator\nParameter 2: Number of epochs\nParameter 3: Sight effect, with values of 0 or 10\nParameter 4: Time samples","category":"page"},{"location":"generated/tutorials/Autoencoder_EEG_Meeting_Minutes/","page":"Autoencoder","title":"Autoencoder","text":"The simulate_data function is used to create both the test and training datasets.","category":"page"},{"location":"generated/tutorials/Autoencoder_EEG_Meeting_Minutes/","page":"Autoencoder","title":"Autoencoder","text":"data_0 and data_10 are the training datasets.\ndata_test_0 and data_test_10 are the testing datasets.","category":"page"},{"location":"generated/tutorials/Autoencoder_EEG_Meeting_Minutes/","page":"Autoencoder","title":"Autoencoder","text":"Running the simulate_data function to create the datasets:","category":"page"},{"location":"generated/tutorials/Autoencoder_EEG_Meeting_Minutes/","page":"Autoencoder","title":"Autoencoder","text":"data, evts = testdata.simulate_data(rng, 100, 1; sfreq=100)\n\n╔═╡ 2d073e94-e75f-4c46-950d-7fa9f4db244a\ndata_0, evts_0 = testdata.simulate_data(rng, 100, 0; sfreq=100)\n\n╔═╡ a3dd7def-6c8c-47dc-8280-933e607e424d\ndata_test_0, evts_test_0 = testdata.simulate_data(rng, 100, 0; sfreq=100)\n\n╔═╡ 374e0d33-0ad9-46ab-beb9-51b7e069ff89\ndata_10, evts_10 = testdata.simulate_data(rng, 100, 10; sfreq=100)\n\n╔═╡ 81a879b6-1077-4044-8195-bcc0d6ce1341\ndata_test_10, evts_test_10 = testdata.simulate_data(rng, 100, 10; sfreq=100)","category":"page"},{"location":"generated/tutorials/Autoencoder_EEG_Meeting_Minutes/","page":"Autoencoder","title":"Autoencoder","text":"The R-squared error ranges from 0 to 1 and measures how closely the predicted values match the actual values. A value closer to 1 indicates that the model's predictions are a good fit with the actual data, while a value closer to 0 suggests a poor fit. In our case, the R-squared value is close to 0 due to the influence of pink noise.","category":"page"},{"location":"generated/tutorials/Autoencoder_EEG_Meeting_Minutes/","page":"Autoencoder","title":"Autoencoder","text":"For the Mean Squared Error (MSE), the ideal value should be close to 0, indicating that the predicted and actual models are a perfect match.","category":"page"},{"location":"generated/tutorials/Autoencoder_EEG_Meeting_Minutes/","page":"Autoencoder","title":"Autoencoder","text":"Additionally, we determined the best fit for the model using a batch size of 256 and 50 hidden channels.","category":"page"},{"location":"generated/tutorials/Autoencoder_EEG_Meeting_Minutes/","page":"Autoencoder","title":"Autoencoder","text":"To test and train the model","category":"page"},{"location":"generated/tutorials/Autoencoder_EEG_Meeting_Minutes/","page":"Autoencoder","title":"Autoencoder","text":"lossepochdata_hearing_0 = []\nlossepochrsquareddata_hearing_0 = []\nloss_test_rsquared_hearing_0 = []\ny_pred_hearing_0 = zeros(Float64, 44, 227, 10)\n\n# Training the model with 0 effect on sight\n# dre,ps, st = fit(DRE, Float32.(data)) # |> CuArray)\ndre, ps, st, lossepochdatahearing_0, lossepochrsquareddatahearing_0 = fit(DRE, Float32.(data_0[:,1:end÷2*2,:]) |> x->use_gpu ? CuArray(x) : x,\n    f_hearing, evts_0; n_epochs=100, mask_percentage=0.1, lr=0.1, batch_size=256, hidden_chs=50) # |> CuArray)\n\n# Testing the model with 0 effect on sight\nl, y_pred_hearing_0[:,:,:] = DeepRecurrentEncoder.test(dre, (Float32.(data_test_0[:,1:end÷2*2,:]) |> x->use_gpu ? CuArray(x) : x),\n    f_hearing, evts_test_0, ps, st; subset_index=1:10, mask_percentage=0.1, loss_function=mse)\n\npush!(lossepochdata_hearing_0, lossepochdatahearing_0)\npush!(lossepochrsquareddata_hearing_0, lossepochrsquareddatahearing_0)\npush!(loss_test_rsquared_hearing_0, l)","category":"page"},{"location":"generated/tutorials/Autoencoder_EEG_Meeting_Minutes/#3.-Graphs-and-Results-Explanation","page":"Autoencoder","title":"3. Graphs and Results Explanation","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"generated/tutorials/Autoencoder_EEG_Meeting_Minutes/","page":"Autoencoder","title":"Autoencoder","text":"Output of Predicted model","category":"page"},{"location":"generated/tutorials/Autoencoder_EEG_Meeting_Minutes/","page":"Autoencoder","title":"Autoencoder","text":"(Image: )","category":"page"},{"location":"generated/tutorials/Autoencoder_EEG_Meeting_Minutes/","page":"Autoencoder","title":"Autoencoder","text":"Output of Actual model","category":"page"},{"location":"generated/tutorials/Autoencoder_EEG_Meeting_Minutes/","page":"Autoencoder","title":"Autoencoder","text":"(Image: )","category":"page"},{"location":"generated/tutorials/Autoencoder_EEG_Meeting_Minutes/","page":"Autoencoder","title":"Autoencoder","text":"The red shape corresponds to a sight effect of 0, while the blue shape corresponds to a sight effect of 10. As we can clearly observe, the blue shape is fuller and resembles a butterfly more due to the larger sight effect.","category":"page"},{"location":"generated/tutorials/Autoencoder_EEG_Meeting_Minutes/","page":"Autoencoder","title":"Autoencoder","text":"(Image: )","category":"page"},{"location":"generated/tutorials/Autoencoder_EEG_Meeting_Minutes/","page":"Autoencoder","title":"Autoencoder","text":"The hearing + sight curve outperforms the hearing-only curve in terms of MSE loss, as the hearing + sight curve is closer to 0.","category":"page"},{"location":"generated/tutorials/Autoencoder_EEG_Meeting_Minutes/","page":"Autoencoder","title":"Autoencoder","text":"(Image: )","category":"page"},{"location":"generated/tutorials/Autoencoder_EEG_Meeting_Minutes/","page":"Autoencoder","title":"Autoencoder","text":"As we can see, the hearing + sight*0 curve is similar to the hearing-only curve. The small differences are due to the randomly generated dataset.","category":"page"},{"location":"generated/tutorials/Autoencoder_EEG_Meeting_Minutes/","page":"Autoencoder","title":"Autoencoder","text":"(Image: )","category":"page"},{"location":"generated/tutorials/Autoencoder_EEG_Meeting_Minutes/","page":"Autoencoder","title":"Autoencoder","text":"Test curve R-square error curves for different hidden channels","category":"page"},{"location":"generated/tutorials/Autoencoder_EEG_Meeting_Minutes/","page":"Autoencoder","title":"Autoencoder","text":"(Image: )","category":"page"},{"location":"generated/tutorials/Autoencoder_EEG_Meeting_Minutes/","page":"Autoencoder","title":"Autoencoder","text":"Predict hearing with the effect value of 10","category":"page"},{"location":"generated/tutorials/Autoencoder_EEG_Meeting_Minutes/","page":"Autoencoder","title":"Autoencoder","text":"(Image: )","category":"page"},{"location":"generated/tutorials/Autoencoder_EEG_Meeting_Minutes/","page":"Autoencoder","title":"Autoencoder","text":"Training curve MSE loss improves with more epochs or more hidden layers, as it gets closer to 0.","category":"page"},{"location":"generated/tutorials/Autoencoder_EEG_Meeting_Minutes/","page":"Autoencoder","title":"Autoencoder","text":"(Image: )","category":"page"},{"location":"generated/tutorials/Autoencoder_EEG_Meeting_Minutes/","page":"Autoencoder","title":"Autoencoder","text":"Training curve R-squared loss improves with more hidden channels or more epochs. The R-squared value hasn't reached 1 due to the presence of pink noise.","category":"page"},{"location":"generated/tutorials/Autoencoder_EEG_Meeting_Minutes/","page":"Autoencoder","title":"Autoencoder","text":"(Image: )","category":"page"},{"location":"generated/tutorials/Autoencoder_EEG_Meeting_Minutes/","page":"Autoencoder","title":"Autoencoder","text":"","category":"page"},{"location":"generated/tutorials/Autoencoder_EEG_Meeting_Minutes/","page":"Autoencoder","title":"Autoencoder","text":"This page was generated using Literate.jl.","category":"page"},{"location":"generated/references/methodReference/","page":"Functions summary","title":"Functions summary","text":"","category":"page"},{"location":"generated/references/methodReference/","page":"Functions summary","title":"Functions summary","text":"Modules = [DeepRecurrentEncoder]","category":"page"},{"location":"generated/references/methodReference/#DeepRecurrentEncoder.DRE","page":"Functions summary","title":"DeepRecurrentEncoder.DRE","text":"DRE{L,E,D}\n\nDefines a Deep Recurrent Encoder (DRE) structure with LSTM, encoder, and decoder components.\n\nFields\n\nlstm_cell::L: The LSTM cell component of the DRE.\nencoder::E: The encoder component of the DRE.\ndecoder::D: The decoder component of the DRE.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"type"},{"location":"generated/references/methodReference/#DeepRecurrentEncoder.DRE-Tuple{Int64, Int64, Int64}","page":"Functions summary","title":"DeepRecurrentEncoder.DRE","text":"Constructs a DRE model with specified input, hidden, and output channels. Current implementation based on publication : arXiv:2103.02339\n\nArguments\n\nin_chs::Int: Number of input channels.\nhidden_chs::Int: Number of hidden channels (filters in convolution and units in LSTM).\nout_chs::Int: Number of output channels.\nkernel_size=4: Kernel size for convolutional layers. Defaults to 4.\nstride=2: Stride for convolutional layers. Defaults to 2.\n\nReturns\n\nA new DRE model instance.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"generated/references/methodReference/#DeepRecurrentEncoder.DRE-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{AbstractArray{T, 3}, NamedTuple, NamedTuple}} where T","page":"Functions summary","title":"DeepRecurrentEncoder.DRE","text":"Applies the DRE model to the input data.\n\nArguments\n\ns::DRE: The DRE model instance.\nx::AbstractArray{T,3}: Input data with three dimensions (time, channel, epoch).\nps::NamedTuple: Parameter states of the model.\nst::NamedTuple: Training states of the model.\n\nReturns\n\ndecoded: The decoded output after applying the model.\nst: Updated states of the model components.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"generated/references/methodReference/#DeepRecurrentEncoder.add_mask!-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{AbstractArray{T, 3}, AbstractArray{T, 3}, Any}} where T","page":"Functions summary","title":"DeepRecurrentEncoder.add_mask!","text":"add_mask!(masked, eeg, p)\n\nIn-place version of add_mask.\n\nArguments\n\nmasked::AbstractArray{T,3}: Array to store masked EEG data.\neeg::AbstractArray{T,3}: Input EEG data of shape (channels, time, epochs).\np::Float64: Proportion of data to mask.\n\nReturns\n\nAbstractArray: The masked array.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"generated/references/methodReference/#DeepRecurrentEncoder.add_mask-Tuple{AbstractArray, Any}","page":"Functions summary","title":"DeepRecurrentEncoder.add_mask","text":"add_mask(eeg, p)\n\nAdd a mask to the EEG data. This is done to help the model control when not to sustain a signal. This is to mainly separate the baseline EEG data from disturbances such as blinking and head movements.  Further information can be obtainer from : https://arxiv.org/pdf/2103.02339\n\nArguments\n\neeg::AbstractArray: The input EEG data of shape (channels, time, epochs).\np::Float64: The proportion of data to mask.\n\nReturns\n\nAbstractArray: A new array containing the EEG data with a mask added.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"generated/references/methodReference/#DeepRecurrentEncoder.add_stimuli!-Tuple{Any, Any, Any}","page":"Functions summary","title":"DeepRecurrentEncoder.add_stimuli!","text":"add_stimuli!(out, in, designmatrix)\n\nIn-place version of add_stimuli.\n\nArguments\n\nout: Output array to store the result.\nin: EEG data of shape (time, channels, epochs).\ndesignmatrix: Matrix describing stimuli for each epoch.\n\nReturns\n\nAbstractArray: The output array with stimuli added.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"generated/references/methodReference/#DeepRecurrentEncoder.add_stimuli-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{AbstractArray{T, 3}, AbstractMatrix{T}}} where T","page":"Functions summary","title":"DeepRecurrentEncoder.add_stimuli","text":"add_stimuli(in, designmatrix)\n\nAdd stimuli information to EEG data. Used to combine EEG signals with stimuli data from the design matrix.\n\nArguments\n\nin::AbstractArray{T,3}: EEG data of shape (time, channels, epochs).\ndesignmatrix::AbstractArray{T,2}: Matrix describing stimuli for each epoch.\n\nReturns\n\nAbstractArray: EEG data with stimuli added.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"generated/references/methodReference/#DeepRecurrentEncoder.compute_loss","page":"Functions summary","title":"DeepRecurrentEncoder.compute_loss","text":"compute_loss(x, y, model, ps, st; loss_function=mse)\n\nCompute the loss for the given inputs, model, and loss function.\n\nArguments\n\nx: Input data.\ny: Ground truth values.\nmodel: Function representing the predictive model.\nps: Model parameters.\nst: Model state.\nloss_function: Loss function to use (default: mse).\n\nReturns\n\n(Float64, AbstractArray, Any): Tuple of loss, predicted values, and updated model state.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"function"},{"location":"generated/references/methodReference/#DeepRecurrentEncoder.create_optimiser","page":"Functions summary","title":"DeepRecurrentEncoder.create_optimiser","text":"Creates an optimizer for training the model.\n\nArguments\n\nps: Model parameters to optimize.\nlr=0.001: Learning rate for the optimizer. Defaults to 0.001.\n\nReturns\n\nA setup optimizer instance using the ADAM algorithm.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"function"},{"location":"generated/references/methodReference/#DeepRecurrentEncoder.generate_designmatrix-Tuple{StatsModels.FormulaTerm, Any}","page":"Functions summary","title":"DeepRecurrentEncoder.generate_designmatrix","text":"generate_designmatrix(f, evts)\n\nGenerate the design matrix for given formula and events. This is to convert a dataset and a formula into a numerical design matrix, making it easier to fit statistical or machine learning models\n\nArguments\n\nf::FormulaTerm: A formula describing the design.\nevts: Events data.\n\nReturns\n\nAbstractArray: The design matrix.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"generated/references/methodReference/#DeepRecurrentEncoder.mse-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"Functions summary","title":"DeepRecurrentEncoder.mse","text":"mse(y_pred, y_true)\n\nCalculate the mean squared error (MSE) between predicted and true values.\n\nArguments\n\ny_pred::AbstractArray: Predicted values.\ny_true::AbstractArray: True values.\n\nReturns\n\nFloat64: The computed MSE.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"generated/references/methodReference/#DeepRecurrentEncoder.prepare_data-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"Functions summary","title":"DeepRecurrentEncoder.prepare_data","text":"Prepares the input data for training by adding masks and stimuli.\n\nArguments\n\ndata: Input data array.\ndesignmatrix: The design matrix to integrate into the input data.\nmask_percentage=0.3: The percentage of data to mask. Defaults to 0.3.\nkwargs...: Additional keyword arguments.\n\nReturns\n\ninput_data: Prepared input data with masks and stimuli added.\ndata: The original data with dimensions potentially permuted.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"generated/references/methodReference/#DeepRecurrentEncoder.r_squared-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"Functions summary","title":"DeepRecurrentEncoder.r_squared","text":"r_squared(y_pred, y_true)\n\nCompute the coefficient of determination (R²) for the given predictions.\n\nArguments\n\ny_pred::AbstractArray: Predicted values.\ny_true::AbstractArray: True values.\n\nReturns\n\nFloat64: The R² score.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"generated/references/methodReference/#DeepRecurrentEncoder.train_test_split","page":"Functions summary","title":"DeepRecurrentEncoder.train_test_split","text":"train_test_split(data, evts; at=0.7)\n\nSplit data and events into training and testing sets.\n\nArguments\n\ndata::AbstractArray: Input data of shape (time, channels, epochs).\nevts::AbstractArray: Events data.\nat::Float64: Proportion of data to include in the training set (default: 0.7).\n\nReturns\n\n(AbstractArray, AbstractArray, AbstractArray, AbstractArray): Training data, training events, testing data, and testing events.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"function"},{"location":"generated/references/methodReference/#StatsAPI.fit-Tuple{Type{DRE}, Vararg{Any}}","page":"Functions summary","title":"StatsAPI.fit","text":"StatsModels.fit(t::Type{DRE}, args...; kwargs...)\n\nThis function provides a shorthand for calling the StatsModels.fit method with a default random number generator (MersenneTwister(1)).\n\n# Arguments\n- `t::Type{DRE}`: The type of the model to fit.\n- `args...`: Additional positional arguments passed to the fitting function.\n- `kwargs...`: Named arguments passed to the fitting function.\n\n# Returns\n- The result of the fitting process using a default RNG.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"generated/tutorials/basicUsage/#Tutorial-:-Using-the-deep-recurrent-encoder","page":"Basic Usage","title":"Tutorial : Using the deep recurrent encoder","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"generated/tutorials/basicUsage/#Overview","page":"Basic Usage","title":"Overview","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"generated/tutorials/basicUsage/","page":"Basic Usage","title":"Basic Usage","text":"This tutorial.jl notebook performs data simulation, trains a deep recurrent encoder model, and visualizes the results. It includes data preparation, model training, and evaluation steps.","category":"page"},{"location":"generated/tutorials/basicUsage/#Dependencies","page":"Basic Usage","title":"Dependencies","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"generated/tutorials/basicUsage/","page":"Basic Usage","title":"Basic Usage","text":"The notebook uses the following Julia packages:","category":"page"},{"location":"generated/tutorials/basicUsage/","page":"Basic Usage","title":"Basic Usage","text":"Pkg for package management\nRandom for random number generation\nPlutoLinks for linking Pluto cells\nLux and LuxCUDA for deep learning (CPU and GPU support)\nCairoMakie and Plots for visualization\nStatistics and StatsModels for statistical modeling\nStableRNGs for reproducibility\nDeepRecurrentEncoder for neural network modeling","category":"page"},{"location":"generated/tutorials/basicUsage/#Notebook-Workflow","page":"Basic Usage","title":"Notebook Workflow","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"generated/tutorials/basicUsage/#1.-Package-Activation","page":"Basic Usage","title":"1. Package Activation","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"generated/tutorials/basicUsage/","page":"Basic Usage","title":"Basic Usage","text":"using Pkg\nPkg.activate(\"../../../.\")","category":"page"},{"location":"generated/tutorials/basicUsage/","page":"Basic Usage","title":"Basic Usage","text":"This activates the package environment located in the /docs directory.","category":"page"},{"location":"generated/tutorials/basicUsage/#2.-Importing-Required-Libraries","page":"Basic Usage","title":"2. Importing Required Libraries","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"generated/tutorials/basicUsage/","page":"Basic Usage","title":"Basic Usage","text":"using Random, PlutoLinks, Lux, LuxCUDA, CairoMakie, Statistics, StatsModels, Plots, StableRNGs","category":"page"},{"location":"generated/tutorials/basicUsage/","page":"Basic Usage","title":"Basic Usage","text":"These libraries are used for data handling, model training, and visualization.","category":"page"},{"location":"generated/tutorials/basicUsage/#3.-Importing-Custom-Deep-Learning-Module","page":"Basic Usage","title":"3. Importing Custom Deep Learning Module","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"generated/tutorials/basicUsage/","page":"Basic Usage","title":"Basic Usage","text":"using DeepRecurrentEncoder","category":"page"},{"location":"generated/tutorials/basicUsage/","page":"Basic Usage","title":"Basic Usage","text":"This imports the DeepRecurrentEncoder module for recurrent neural network training. The @revise tracks for changes done in the module to refresh the cells in the notebook","category":"page"},{"location":"generated/tutorials/basicUsage/#4.-Loading-Test-Data","page":"Basic Usage","title":"4. Loading Test Data","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"generated/tutorials/basicUsage/","page":"Basic Usage","title":"Basic Usage","text":"include(\"../../../testdata.jl\")","category":"page"},{"location":"generated/tutorials/basicUsage/","page":"Basic Usage","title":"Basic Usage","text":"This loads data simulation functionalities from provided by Unfold.jl","category":"page"},{"location":"generated/tutorials/basicUsage/#5.-Random-Number-Generator","page":"Basic Usage","title":"5. Random Number Generator","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"generated/tutorials/basicUsage/","page":"Basic Usage","title":"Basic Usage","text":"rng = StableRNG(1)","category":"page"},{"location":"generated/tutorials/basicUsage/","page":"Basic Usage","title":"Basic Usage","text":"A stable random number generator to simulate data from the UnfoldSim.jl package (<Link to unfold sim>) ","category":"page"},{"location":"generated/tutorials/basicUsage/#6.-Defining-Formula-for-Model","page":"Basic Usage","title":"6. Defining Formula for Model","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"generated/tutorials/basicUsage/","page":"Basic Usage","title":"Basic Usage","text":"f = @formula 0 ~ 0 + sight + hearing","category":"page"},{"location":"generated/tutorials/basicUsage/","page":"Basic Usage","title":"Basic Usage","text":"Defines a statistical formula to define the stimuli effects given to the model","category":"page"},{"location":"generated/tutorials/basicUsage/#7.-Simulating-Data","page":"Basic Usage","title":"7. Simulating Data","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"generated/tutorials/basicUsage/","page":"Basic Usage","title":"Basic Usage","text":"data, evts = simulate_data(rng, 100; sfreq=100, sight_effect=1);","category":"page"},{"location":"generated/tutorials/basicUsage/","page":"Basic Usage","title":"Basic Usage","text":"Simulates samples of data using the random number generator and a given sight_effect. This controls on how much the \"sight\" stimulus influences the final EEG signal","category":"page"},{"location":"generated/tutorials/basicUsage/#8.-GPU-Toggle","page":"Basic Usage","title":"8. GPU Toggle","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"generated/tutorials/basicUsage/","page":"Basic Usage","title":"Basic Usage","text":"use_gpu = false","category":"page"},{"location":"generated/tutorials/basicUsage/","page":"Basic Usage","title":"Basic Usage","text":"A boolean flag to determine whether to use a GPU for training.","category":"page"},{"location":"generated/tutorials/basicUsage/#9.-Model-Training","page":"Basic Usage","title":"9. Model Training","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"generated/tutorials/basicUsage/","page":"Basic Usage","title":"Basic Usage","text":"x_train_data, train_evts, x_test_data, test_evts = train_test_split(data, evts)\ndata_input = Float32.(x_train_data[:, 1:end÷2*2, :]) |> x -> use_gpu ? CuArray(x) : x\nloss = MSELoss()\ndre, ps, st, loss_train, loss_opt = fit(DRE, data_input, f, train_evts; n_epochs=10, lr=0.1, batch_size=256, loss_opt=loss, hidden_chs=25)","category":"page"},{"location":"generated/tutorials/basicUsage/","page":"Basic Usage","title":"Basic Usage","text":"Splits the data into training and test sets.\nConverts training data to Float32 format and optionally moves it to GPU (in case use_gpu) is true\nDefines mean squared error loss (Customizable).\nTrains the deep recurrent encoder model using given epochs, a given  learning rate, and a given batch size.","category":"page"},{"location":"generated/tutorials/basicUsage/#10.-Model-Testing","page":"Basic Usage","title":"10. Model Testing","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"generated/tutorials/basicUsage/","page":"Basic Usage","title":"Basic Usage","text":"data_test = Float32.(x_test_data[:, 1:end÷2*2, :])\nloss_pred, data_pred = DeepRecurrentEncoder.test(dre, data_test, f, test_evts, ps, st; subset_index=1:10, loss_function=mse)","category":"page"},{"location":"generated/tutorials/basicUsage/","page":"Basic Usage","title":"Basic Usage","text":"Converts test data to Float32 format.\nEvaluates the trained model on the test dataset.","category":"page"},{"location":"generated/tutorials/basicUsage/#11.-Visualization","page":"Basic Usage","title":"11. Visualization","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"generated/tutorials/basicUsage/#Input-Data-Visualization","page":"Basic Usage","title":"Input Data Visualization","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"generated/tutorials/basicUsage/","page":"Basic Usage","title":"Basic Usage","text":"series((data_test[:, :, 10]); solid_color=:black)","category":"page"},{"location":"generated/tutorials/basicUsage/#Prediction-Visualization","page":"Basic Usage","title":"Prediction Visualization","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"generated/tutorials/basicUsage/","page":"Basic Usage","title":"Basic Usage","text":"series(data_pred[:, :, 10]', solid_color=:black)","category":"page"},{"location":"generated/tutorials/basicUsage/#Training-Loss-Visualization","page":"Basic Usage","title":"Training Loss Visualization","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"generated/tutorials/basicUsage/","page":"Basic Usage","title":"Basic Usage","text":"f_ = Figure()\nax1_ = Axis(f_[1, 1], xlabel=\"Epochs\", ylabel=\"error\", title=\"Error vs epochs\")\nlines!(ax1_, loss_train, label=\"loss\", color=:red)\naxislegend(ax1_)\nf_","category":"page"},{"location":"generated/tutorials/basicUsage/","page":"Basic Usage","title":"Basic Usage","text":"Creates a figure to plot training loss over epochs.\nUses CairoMakie to visualize the error reduction.","category":"page"},{"location":"generated/tutorials/basicUsage/#Summary","page":"Basic Usage","title":"Summary","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"generated/tutorials/basicUsage/","page":"Basic Usage","title":"Basic Usage","text":"This notebook:","category":"page"},{"location":"generated/tutorials/basicUsage/","page":"Basic Usage","title":"Basic Usage","text":"Loads and simulates time-series data.\nDefines and trains a deep recurrent encoder model.\nEvaluates the model on test data.\nVisualizes the input data, predictions, and training loss trends.","category":"page"},{"location":"generated/explanations/installation/#Installing-Julia-and-DeepRecurrentEncoder.jl","page":"Installation","title":"Installing Julia & DeepRecurrentEncoder.jl","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"generated/explanations/installation/#Installing-Julia","page":"Installation","title":"Installing Julia","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"generated/explanations/installation/","page":"Installation","title":"Installation","text":"The recommended way to install julia is juliaup.","category":"page"},{"location":"generated/explanations/installation/","page":"Installation","title":"Installation","text":"TL;DR: If you don't want to read the explicit instructions, just copy the following command:","category":"page"},{"location":"generated/explanations/installation/","page":"Installation","title":"Installation","text":"Windows: winget install julia -s msstore\nMac/Linux: curl -fsSL https://install.julialang.org | sh","category":"page"},{"location":"generated/explanations/installation/","page":"Installation","title":"Installation","text":"We further recommend to use VSCode and install the Julia Extension.","category":"page"},{"location":"generated/explanations/installation/#Installing-DeepRecurrentEncoder.jl","page":"Installation","title":"Installing DeepRecurrentEncoder.jl","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"generated/explanations/installation/","page":"Installation","title":"Installation","text":"The following instructions are intended for Julia beginners. More advanced Julia users can jump ahead to step 3.","category":"page"},{"location":"generated/explanations/installation/#1.-Start-an-interactive-Julia-session-(\"REPL\")","page":"Installation","title":"1. Start an interactive Julia session (\"REPL\")","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"generated/explanations/installation/","page":"Installation","title":"Installation","text":"Option 1: Type julia in the command line.\nOption 2: In VSCode, press Ctrl + Shift + P to open the command palette and type in Julia: Start REPL.","category":"page"},{"location":"generated/explanations/installation/#2.-Activate-your-project-environment","page":"Installation","title":"2. Activate your project environment","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"generated/explanations/installation/","page":"Installation","title":"Installation","text":"Before installing DeepRecurrentEncode.jl make sure that you activated your project environment.","category":"page"},{"location":"generated/explanations/installation/","page":"Installation","title":"Installation","text":"Option 1: cd(\"/path/to/your/project\") and ]activate .\nOption 2: ]activate /path/to/your/project/","category":"page"},{"location":"generated/explanations/installation/","page":"Installation","title":"Installation","text":"hint: Hint\nAfter activating you should see (environment) pkg> (where environment is the name of your project folder). If you see (@v1.11) pkg> instead, you still have to activate your environment.","category":"page"},{"location":"generated/explanations/installation/","page":"Installation","title":"Installation","text":"Note that by typing ] you enter the Julia package manager. To get back to the Julia REPL, press backspace.","category":"page"},{"location":"generated/explanations/installation/#3.-Install-the-DeepRecurrentEncoder.jl-package","page":"Installation","title":"3. Install the DeepRecurrentEncoder.jl package","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"generated/explanations/installation/","page":"Installation","title":"Installation","text":"If you are not in the package manager anymore, type ].\nThen type add DeepRecurrentEncoder.","category":"page"},{"location":"generated/explanations/installation/","page":"Installation","title":"Installation","text":"After the installation is finished you can use using DeepRecurrentEncoder in the REPL to import the package.","category":"page"},{"location":"generated/explanations/introduction/#Introduction","page":"Basics","title":"Introduction","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"generated/explanations/introduction/","page":"Basics","title":"Basics","text":"This package describes the implementation and usage of a deep recurrent encoder used to encode complex EEG signals into an embedded representation with much lesser data quantity.","category":"page"},{"location":"generated/explanations/introduction/","page":"Basics","title":"Basics","text":"Let’s explore each aspect of this in detail.  ","category":"page"},{"location":"generated/explanations/introduction/","page":"Basics","title":"Basics","text":"","category":"page"},{"location":"generated/explanations/introduction/#What-is-EEG?","page":"Basics","title":"What is EEG?","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"generated/explanations/introduction/","page":"Basics","title":"Basics","text":"Electroencephalography (EEG) signals represent the electrical activity of the brain, captured through electrodes placed on the scalp. These signals arise from the synchronized firing of neurons, particularly the postsynaptic potentials of cortical pyramidal cells. EEG is a valuable tool for studying brain function, diagnosing neurological conditions, and exploring cognitive processes.","category":"page"},{"location":"generated/explanations/introduction/#Characteristics-of-EEG-Signals","page":"Basics","title":"Characteristics of EEG Signals","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"generated/explanations/introduction/","page":"Basics","title":"Basics","text":"Amplitude and Frequency: EEG signals are typically low in amplitude, ranging from a few microvolts to hundreds of microvolts. Their frequency is categorized into bands such as:\nDelta (0.5–4 Hz): Associated with deep sleep and unconscious states.\nTheta (4–8 Hz): Linked to light sleep, relaxation, and creativity.\nAlpha (8–12 Hz): Indicates calmness and relaxed wakefulness.\nBeta (13–30 Hz): Reflects active thinking and alertness.\nGamma (30+ Hz): Related to high-level cognitive functioning, such as problem-solving and memory.\nTemporal and Spatial Resolution: EEG provides high temporal resolution, capturing rapid brain activity in milliseconds. However, its spatial resolution is limited, as the signals reflect activity across broader cortical areas rather than specific deep brain regions.","category":"page"},{"location":"generated/explanations/introduction/#Applications-of-EEG","page":"Basics","title":"Applications of EEG","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"generated/explanations/introduction/#Medical-Diagnosis:","page":"Basics","title":"Medical Diagnosis:","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"generated/explanations/introduction/","page":"Basics","title":"Basics","text":"Commonly used to diagnose conditions like epilepsy, where abnormal brain wave patterns are evident.\nAssists in detecting sleep disorders, such as insomnia or sleep apnea.\nSupports the evaluation of coma states and brain death.","category":"page"},{"location":"generated/explanations/introduction/#Neuroscientific-Research:","page":"Basics","title":"Neuroscientific Research:","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"generated/explanations/introduction/","page":"Basics","title":"Basics","text":"Studies cognition, sensory processing, and emotional responses.\nUsed in brain-computer interface (BCI) systems to enable control of devices through thought patterns.","category":"page"},{"location":"generated/explanations/introduction/#Psychological-and-Cognitive-Applications:","page":"Basics","title":"Psychological and Cognitive Applications:","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"generated/explanations/introduction/","page":"Basics","title":"Basics","text":"Helps analyze stress, mental workload, and relaxation levels.\nInvestigates disorders like ADHD and autism through distinct EEG patterns.","category":"page"},{"location":"generated/explanations/introduction/","page":"Basics","title":"Basics","text":"","category":"page"},{"location":"generated/explanations/introduction/#What-is-an-Autoencoder?","page":"Basics","title":"What is an Autoencoder?","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"generated/explanations/introduction/","page":"Basics","title":"Basics","text":"An autoencoder is a type of artificial neural network designed for unsupervised learning, primarily used for dimensionality reduction, feature learning, and data denoising. It works by compressing input data into a smaller, latent representation and then reconstructing it to be as close to the original as possible.","category":"page"},{"location":"generated/explanations/introduction/#Key-Components:","page":"Basics","title":"Key Components:","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"generated/explanations/introduction/","page":"Basics","title":"Basics","text":"Encoder: Maps the input data to a lower-dimensional latent space.\nLatent Space: A compressed representation of the input, capturing its most significant features.\nDecoder: Reconstructs the input data from the latent representation.","category":"page"},{"location":"generated/explanations/introduction/#Applications:","page":"Basics","title":"Applications:","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"generated/explanations/introduction/","page":"Basics","title":"Basics","text":"Dimensionality Reduction: Reduces complex data to fewer dimensions for visualization or preprocessing.\nDenoising: Removes noise from data, such as images or signals.\nAnomaly Detection: Identifies unusual patterns by comparing input to reconstructed output.","category":"page"},{"location":"generated/explanations/introduction/","page":"Basics","title":"Basics","text":"The diagram above illustrates how an autoencoder works: input data is passed through the encoder to the bottleneck (latent space) and then reconstructed by the decoder.","category":"page"},{"location":"generated/explanations/introduction/","page":"Basics","title":"Basics","text":"","category":"page"},{"location":"generated/explanations/introduction/#Why-do-we-need-an-Autoencoder?","page":"Basics","title":"Why do we need an Autoencoder?","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"generated/explanations/introduction/","page":"Basics","title":"Basics","text":"EEG signals in real life are presented in channels, each channel corresponding to a specific electrode placed on the scalp. More frequently, these may be in the order of more than 150-200 channels. This amount of data is very large and highly complex to process for any application. Hence, we use an autoencoder to learn a smaller latent space representation of the data, reducing computational complexity in later stages.","category":"page"},{"location":"generated/explanations/introduction/#Architecture:","page":"Basics","title":"Architecture:","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"generated/explanations/introduction/","page":"Basics","title":"Basics","text":"The 200 channels of EEG signals are fed into a deep recurrent network. This is a recurrent network since we need to encode not only the data in the current timestamp but also previous states.\nThe neural network compresses the input data into a latent space of a size pre-specified by the designer. The higher the latent space dimension, the better the accuracy but at the cost of increased space complexity.\nUsing this latent space, the decoder attempts to reconstruct the original signal. The model learns the most accurate latent space representation, maximizing captured information.","category":"page"},{"location":"generated/explanations/introduction/","page":"Basics","title":"Basics","text":"","category":"page"},{"location":"generated/explanations/introduction/#About-the-DRE-Package","page":"Basics","title":"About the DRE Package","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"generated/explanations/introduction/","page":"Basics","title":"Basics","text":"The current implementation of the Deep Recurrent Encoder (DRE) model is as follows:","category":"page"},{"location":"generated/explanations/introduction/","page":"Basics","title":"Basics","text":"function DRE(in_chs::Int, hidden_chs::Int, out_chs::Int; kernel_size=4, stride=2)\n\n    return DRE(\n        \n        LSTMCell(hidden_chs => hidden_chs),\n\n        Conv((kernel_size,), (in_chs => hidden_chs), identity, stride=(stride,), use_bias=true, pad=SamePad()),\n\n        ConvTranspose((kernel_size,), (hidden_chs => out_chs), identity, stride=(stride,), use_bias=true, pad=SamePad())\n\n    )\n\nend","category":"page"},{"location":"generated/howto/stimuliEffect/#How-to-change-stimuli","page":"Effect of stimuli input","title":"How to change stimuli","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"generated/howto/stimuliEffect/#Overview","page":"Effect of stimuli input","title":"Overview","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"generated/howto/stimuliEffect/","page":"Effect of stimuli input","title":"Effect of stimuli input","text":"This notebook implements a deep recurrent encoder for analyzing sensory stimuli (sight, hearing, and combined) using the Lux library in Julia. The data is simulated and then trained under three conditions: sight-only, hearing-only, and combined stimuli. Results are plotted to show the differences in predictions. The executable pluto notebook can be found in the research folder under stimulieffect.jl","category":"page"},{"location":"generated/howto/stimuliEffect/#Setup","page":"Effect of stimuli input","title":"Setup","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"generated/howto/stimuliEffect/#Activating-Environment","page":"Effect of stimuli input","title":"Activating Environment","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"generated/howto/stimuliEffect/","page":"Effect of stimuli input","title":"Effect of stimuli input","text":"using Pkg\nPkg.activate(\"../../../.\")","category":"page"},{"location":"generated/howto/stimuliEffect/","page":"Effect of stimuli input","title":"Effect of stimuli input","text":"Activates the Julia environment located at ../../docs.","category":"page"},{"location":"generated/howto/stimuliEffect/#Importing-Packages","page":"Effect of stimuli input","title":"Importing Packages","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"generated/howto/stimuliEffect/","page":"Effect of stimuli input","title":"Effect of stimuli input","text":"using Random\nusing PlutoLinks\nusing Lux\nusing LuxCUDA\nusing CairoMakie\nusing Statistics\nusing StatsModels\nusing Plots\nusing StableRNGs\nusing DataFrames","category":"page"},{"location":"generated/howto/stimuliEffect/","page":"Effect of stimuli input","title":"Effect of stimuli input","text":"Loads the required libraries for neural network modeling, plotting, and statistical analysis.","category":"page"},{"location":"generated/howto/stimuliEffect/#Importing-Custom-Encoder","page":"Effect of stimuli input","title":"Importing Custom Encoder","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"generated/howto/stimuliEffect/","page":"Effect of stimuli input","title":"Effect of stimuli input","text":"using DeepRecurrentEncoder","category":"page"},{"location":"generated/howto/stimuliEffect/","page":"Effect of stimuli input","title":"Effect of stimuli input","text":"Imports the DeepRecurrentEncoder module with automatic reloading of changes.","category":"page"},{"location":"generated/howto/stimuliEffect/#Loading-Test-Data","page":"Effect of stimuli input","title":"Loading Test Data","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"generated/howto/stimuliEffect/","page":"Effect of stimuli input","title":"Effect of stimuli input","text":"include(\"../../../testdata.jl\")","category":"page"},{"location":"generated/howto/stimuliEffect/","page":"Effect of stimuli input","title":"Effect of stimuli input","text":"Loads the test dataset from testdata.jl.","category":"page"},{"location":"generated/howto/stimuliEffect/#Setting-Random-Seed","page":"Effect of stimuli input","title":"Setting Random Seed","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"generated/howto/stimuliEffect/","page":"Effect of stimuli input","title":"Effect of stimuli input","text":"rng = StableRNG(1)","category":"page"},{"location":"generated/howto/stimuliEffect/","page":"Effect of stimuli input","title":"Effect of stimuli input","text":"Define a random number generator for the data generation module","category":"page"},{"location":"generated/howto/stimuliEffect/#Defining-Formulas","page":"Effect of stimuli input","title":"Defining Formulas","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"generated/howto/stimuliEffect/","page":"Effect of stimuli input","title":"Effect of stimuli input","text":"f_hearing = @formula 0 ~ 0 + hearing\nf_sight = @formula 0 ~ 0 + sight\nf_combined = @formula 0 ~ 0 + sight + hearing","category":"page"},{"location":"generated/howto/stimuliEffect/","page":"Effect of stimuli input","title":"Effect of stimuli input","text":"Defines formulas to model the effect of sight, hearing, and combined stimuli on the response variable.","category":"page"},{"location":"generated/howto/stimuliEffect/#Simulating-Data","page":"Effect of stimuli input","title":"Simulating Data","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"generated/howto/stimuliEffect/","page":"Effect of stimuli input","title":"Effect of stimuli input","text":"data, evts = simulate_data(rng, 100; sfreq=100, sight_effect=1);","category":"page"},{"location":"generated/howto/stimuliEffect/","page":"Effect of stimuli input","title":"Effect of stimuli input","text":"Simulates data with a customizable sight effect.","category":"page"},{"location":"generated/howto/stimuliEffect/#Training-Models","page":"Effect of stimuli input","title":"Training Models","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"generated/howto/stimuliEffect/","page":"Effect of stimuli input","title":"Effect of stimuli input","text":"Enable/Disable GPU usage.","category":"page"},{"location":"generated/howto/stimuliEffect/","page":"Effect of stimuli input","title":"Effect of stimuli input","text":"use_gpu = false","category":"page"},{"location":"generated/howto/stimuliEffect/","page":"Effect of stimuli input","title":"Effect of stimuli input","text":"Each block trains a DeepRecurrentEncoder model for each stimulus type. The model is trained for 20 epochs with a learning rate of 0.01 and a batch size of 256.","category":"page"},{"location":"generated/howto/stimuliEffect/#Training-with-Sight-Stimulus","page":"Effect of stimuli input","title":"Training with Sight Stimulus","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"generated/howto/stimuliEffect/","page":"Effect of stimuli input","title":"Effect of stimuli input","text":"dre_sight, ps_sight, st_sight, loss_epoch_data_sight, loss_epoch_opt_data_sight = fit(DRE, Float32.(data[:, 1:end÷2*2, :]) |> x -> use_gpu ? CuArray(x) : x, f_sight, evts; n_epochs=20, lr=0.01, batch_size=256, hidden_chs=100)\nloss_test_opt_sight, y_pred_sight = DeepRecurrentEncoder.test(dre_sight, (Float32.(data[:, 1:end÷2*2, :]) |> x -> use_gpu ? CuArray(x) : x), f_sight, evts, ps_sight, st_sight; subset_index=1:10, loss_function=mse)","category":"page"},{"location":"generated/howto/stimuliEffect/#Training-with-Hearing-Stimulus","page":"Effect of stimuli input","title":"Training with Hearing Stimulus","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"generated/howto/stimuliEffect/","page":"Effect of stimuli input","title":"Effect of stimuli input","text":"dre_hearing, ps_hearing, st_hearing, loss_epoch_data_hearing, loss_epoch_opt_data_hearing = fit(DRE, Float32.(data[:, 1:end÷2*2, :]) |> x -> use_gpu ? CuArray(x) : x, f_hearing, evts; n_epochs=20, lr=0.01, batch_size=256, hidden_chs=100)\nloss_test_opt_hearing, y_pred_hearing = DeepRecurrentEncoder.test(dre_hearing, (Float32.(data[:, 1:end÷2*2, :]) |> x -> use_gpu ? CuArray(x) : x), f_hearing, evts, ps_hearing, st_hearing; subset_index=1:10, loss_function=mse)","category":"page"},{"location":"generated/howto/stimuliEffect/#Training-with-both-Stimuli","page":"Effect of stimuli input","title":"Training with both Stimuli","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"generated/howto/stimuliEffect/","page":"Effect of stimuli input","title":"Effect of stimuli input","text":"dre_combined, ps_combined, st_combined, loss_epoch_data_combined, loss_epoch_opt_data_combined = fit(DRE, Float32.(data[:, 1:end÷2*2, :]) |> x -> use_gpu ? CuArray(x) : x, f_combined, evts; n_epochs=20, lr=0.01, batch_size=256, hidden_chs=100)\nloss_test_opt_combined, y_pred_combined = DeepRecurrentEncoder.test(dre_combined, (Float32.(data[:, 1:end÷2*2, :]) |> x -> use_gpu ? CuArray(x) : x), f_combined, evts, ps_combined, st_combined; subset_index=1:10, loss_function=mse)","category":"page"},{"location":"generated/howto/stimuliEffect/","page":"Effect of stimuli input","title":"Effect of stimuli input","text":"(Similar blocks exist for hearing and combined stimuli.)","category":"page"},{"location":"generated/howto/stimuliEffect/#Results-Summary","page":"Effect of stimuli input","title":"Results Summary","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"generated/howto/stimuliEffect/","page":"Effect of stimuli input","title":"Effect of stimuli input","text":"df = DataFrame(\n    Stimulus = [\"Sight\", \"Hearing\", \"Combined\"],\n    Training_Loss = [loss_epoch_data_sight[1][end], loss_epoch_data_hearing[1][end], loss_epoch_data_combined[1][end]],\n    Testing_Loss = [loss_test_opt_sight[1], loss_test_opt_hearing[1], loss_test_opt_combined[1]],\n    R²_Score = [mean(loss_epoch_opt_data_sight[1]), mean(loss_epoch_opt_data_hearing[1]), mean(loss_epoch_opt_data_combined[1])]\n)","category":"page"},{"location":"generated/howto/stimuliEffect/","page":"Effect of stimuli input","title":"Effect of stimuli input","text":"Prints the summary of training loss, testing loss, and R² score for each trained model.","category":"page"},{"location":"generated/howto/stimuliEffect/#Error-vs-Epochs","page":"Effect of stimuli input","title":"Error vs Epochs","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"generated/howto/stimuliEffect/","page":"Effect of stimuli input","title":"Effect of stimuli input","text":"f_ = Figure()\nax1_ = Axis(f_[1,1], xlabel=\"Epochs\", ylabel=\"error\", title=\"Error vs epochs\")\nlines!(ax1_, loss_epoch_data_sight, label=\"sight\", color=:red)\nlines!(ax1_, (loss_epoch_data_hearing), label=\"hearing\", color=:blue)\nlines!(ax1_, (loss_epoch_data_combined), label=\"combined\", color=:black)\naxislegend(ax1_)\nf_","category":"page"},{"location":"generated/howto/stimuliEffect/#Conclusion","page":"Effect of stimuli input","title":"Conclusion","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"generated/howto/stimuliEffect/","page":"Effect of stimuli input","title":"Effect of stimuli input","text":"Three models were trained to predict sensory responses to sight, hearing, and combined stimuli.\nResults show varying loss and R² scores for each stimulus, with combined stimuli performing differently.\nVisualizations provide insights into predictions and training performance.","category":"page"},{"location":"generated/tutorials/gpUsage/#Tutorial-How-to-run-models-on-a-GPU","page":"Training on a GPU","title":"Tutorial - How to run models on a GPU","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"generated/tutorials/gpUsage/","page":"Training on a GPU","title":"Training on a GPU","text":"Julia has first-class support for GPU programming. And hence, you can train the models for the DeepRecurrentEncoder directly on a GPU","category":"page"},{"location":"generated/tutorials/gpUsage/","page":"Training on a GPU","title":"Training on a GPU","text":"To do this, just cast the data that you want to train into a GPU array class, and you're all good to go","category":"page"},{"location":"generated/tutorials/gpUsage/","page":"Training on a GPU","title":"Training on a GPU","text":"For example, ","category":"page"},{"location":"generated/tutorials/gpUsage/","page":"Training on a GPU","title":"Training on a GPU","text":"dre, ps, st, loss_epoch, loss_epoch_rsquared = fit(DRE, Float32.(data[:,1:end÷2*2,:])|> x-> CuArray(x),f,evts;n_epochs=25,lr=0.1,batch_size=256, hidden_chs = hidden_channels[k])","category":"page"},{"location":"generated/tutorials/gpUsage/","page":"Training on a GPU","title":"Training on a GPU","text":"As you can see, the statement x -> CuArray(x) casts the array into a CudaArray (Which is an instance of a GPU array), and hence, the training of the model happens on a GPU.","category":"page"},{"location":"generated/tutorials/gpUsage/","page":"Training on a GPU","title":"Training on a GPU","text":"Alternatively, you can just use a variable use_gpu to toggle GPU usage or not","category":"page"},{"location":"generated/tutorials/gpUsage/","page":"Training on a GPU","title":"Training on a GPU","text":"use_gpu = true\ndre, ps, st, loss_epoch, loss_epoch_rsquared = fit(DRE, Float32.(data[:,1:end÷2*2,:])|> x->use_gpu ? CuArray(x) : x,f,evts;n_epochs=25,lr=0.1,batch_size=256, hidden_chs = hidden_channels[k])","category":"page"},{"location":"","page":"Home","title":"Home","text":"CurrentModule = DeepRecurrentEncoder","category":"page"},{"location":"#DeepRecurrentEncoder","page":"Home","title":"DeepRecurrentEncoder","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"","page":"Home","title":"Home","text":"Documentation for DeepRecurrentEncoder.","category":"page"},{"location":"","page":"Home","title":"Home","text":"Welcome to DeepRecurrentEncoder.jl: a Julia package to Train and test a deep recurrent encoder with ease","category":"page"},{"location":"#Installation","page":"Home","title":"Installation","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"","page":"Home","title":"Home","text":"To install the package, just open the Julia REPL and type in","category":"page"},{"location":"","page":"Home","title":"Home","text":"using Pkg; Pkg.add(\"DeepRecurrentEncoder\")","category":"page"},{"location":"#Where-to-start:-Learning-roadmap","page":"Home","title":"Where to start: Learning roadmap","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"#0.-First-first-steps","page":"Home","title":"0. First first steps","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"","page":"Home","title":"Home","text":"📌 Goal: Familiarize yourself with EEG signals and Auto encoders.\n🔗 EEG signals | Auto Encoderes","category":"page"},{"location":"#1.-First-steps","page":"Home","title":"1. First steps","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"","page":"Home","title":"Home","text":"📌 Goal: Learn to fit a model with DeepRecurrentEncoder package\n🔗 Tutorial for basic usage","category":"page"},{"location":"#2.-Intermediate-topics","page":"Home","title":"2. Intermediate topics","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"","page":"Home","title":"Home","text":"📌 Goal: Explore effect of hidden channels and check out how to change stimuli inputs on the encoder\n🔗 Effect of hidden channels | Changing stimuli","category":"page"},{"location":"","page":"Home","title":"Home","text":"You should also Read the paper on which the current version of the model is based on Link to article. Enjoy!","category":"page"},{"location":"","page":"Home","title":"Home","text":"<!---\nNote: The statement of need is also used in the `README.md`. Make sure that they are synchronized.\n-->","category":"page"},{"location":"generated/references/hiddenChannelsEffect/#Effect-of-hidden-channels-on-deep-recurrent-encoder","page":"Effect of hidden channels","title":"Effect of hidden channels on deep recurrent encoder","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"generated/references/hiddenChannelsEffect/#Overview","page":"Effect of hidden channels","title":"Overview","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"generated/references/hiddenChannelsEffect/","page":"Effect of hidden channels","title":"Effect of hidden channels","text":"This document provides an overview of a hidden_chs.jl notebook that utilizes deep recurrent encoding techniques with various hidden channel configurations. It demonstrates data simulation, model fitting, and visualization of results.","category":"page"},{"location":"generated/references/hiddenChannelsEffect/","page":"Effect of hidden channels","title":"Effect of hidden channels","text":"","category":"page"},{"location":"generated/references/hiddenChannelsEffect/#Dependencies","page":"Effect of hidden channels","title":"Dependencies","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"generated/references/hiddenChannelsEffect/#Julia-Packages-Used","page":"Effect of hidden channels","title":"Julia Packages Used","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"generated/references/hiddenChannelsEffect/","page":"Effect of hidden channels","title":"Effect of hidden channels","text":"using Pkg\nPkg.activate(\"../../../.\")","category":"page"},{"location":"generated/references/hiddenChannelsEffect/","page":"Effect of hidden channels","title":"Effect of hidden channels","text":"using Random\nusing PlutoLinks\nusing Lux\nusing LuxCUDA\nusing CairoMakie\nusing Statistics\nusing StatsModels\nusing StableRNGs","category":"page"},{"location":"generated/references/hiddenChannelsEffect/","page":"Effect of hidden channels","title":"Effect of hidden channels","text":"using DeepRecurrentEncoder","category":"page"},{"location":"generated/references/hiddenChannelsEffect/#Data-Preparation","page":"Effect of hidden channels","title":"Data Preparation","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"generated/references/hiddenChannelsEffect/#Load-package-to-generate-test-data","page":"Effect of hidden channels","title":"Load package to generate test data","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"generated/references/hiddenChannelsEffect/","page":"Effect of hidden channels","title":"Effect of hidden channels","text":"include(\"../../../testdata.jl\")","category":"page"},{"location":"generated/references/hiddenChannelsEffect/","page":"Effect of hidden channels","title":"Effect of hidden channels","text":"Define a random number generator for the data generation module","category":"page"},{"location":"generated/references/hiddenChannelsEffect/","page":"Effect of hidden channels","title":"Effect of hidden channels","text":"rng = StableRNG(1)","category":"page"},{"location":"generated/references/hiddenChannelsEffect/#Define-Statistical-formula-for-stimuli-data","page":"Effect of hidden channels","title":"Define Statistical formula for stimuli data","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"generated/references/hiddenChannelsEffect/","page":"Effect of hidden channels","title":"Effect of hidden channels","text":"f = @formula 0 ~ 0 + sight + hearing","category":"page"},{"location":"generated/references/hiddenChannelsEffect/#Simulate-Data","page":"Effect of hidden channels","title":"Simulate Data","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"generated/references/hiddenChannelsEffect/","page":"Effect of hidden channels","title":"Effect of hidden channels","text":"data, evts = simulate_data(rng, 100; sfreq=100, sight_effect=1);","category":"page"},{"location":"generated/references/hiddenChannelsEffect/#Model-Training","page":"Effect of hidden channels","title":"Model Training","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"generated/references/hiddenChannelsEffect/#Define-Parameters","page":"Effect of hidden channels","title":"Define Parameters","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"generated/references/hiddenChannelsEffect/","page":"Effect of hidden channels","title":"Effect of hidden channels","text":"Defining some variables to hold the results of training and testing","category":"page"},{"location":"generated/references/hiddenChannelsEffect/","page":"Effect of hidden channels","title":"Effect of hidden channels","text":"use_gpu = false","category":"page"},{"location":"generated/references/hiddenChannelsEffect/","page":"Effect of hidden channels","title":"Effect of hidden channels","text":"Enable/disaible GPU usage","category":"page"},{"location":"generated/references/hiddenChannelsEffect/","page":"Effect of hidden channels","title":"Effect of hidden channels","text":"lossepochdata = []\nlossepochrsquareddata = []\nloss_test_rsquared = []\nhidden_channels = [10,25,50,75,100]\ny_pred = zeros(5, 44, 227, 100)","category":"page"},{"location":"generated/references/hiddenChannelsEffect/#Train-Model-with-Different-Hidden-Channels","page":"Effect of hidden channels","title":"Train Model with Different Hidden Channels","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"generated/references/hiddenChannelsEffect/","page":"Effect of hidden channels","title":"Effect of hidden channels","text":"This trains a Deep recurrent encoder for different hidden channel configurations. Currently, the model is being tested for hidden channels [10, 25, 50, 75, 100]","category":"page"},{"location":"generated/references/hiddenChannelsEffect/","page":"Effect of hidden channels","title":"Effect of hidden channels","text":"for k in 1:5\n\tdre,ps, st, loss_epoch_data, loss_epoch_rsquared_data = fit(DRE, Float32.(data[:,1:end÷2*2,:])|> x->use_gpu ? CuArray(x) : x,f,evts;n_epochs=25,lr=0.1,batch_size=256, hidden_chs = hidden_channels[k])\n\tl,y_pred[k,:,:,:] = DeepRecurrentEncoder.test(dre,(Float32.(data[:,1:end÷2*2,:])|> x->use_gpu ? CuArray(x) : x),f,evts,ps,st;subset_index=1:100,loss_function = mse)\n\tpush!(lossepochdata, loss_epoch_data)\n\tpush!(lossepochrsquareddata, loss_epoch_rsquared_data)\n\tpush!(loss_test_rsquared,l)\nend","category":"page"},{"location":"generated/references/hiddenChannelsEffect/","page":"Effect of hidden channels","title":"Effect of hidden channels","text":"","category":"page"},{"location":"generated/references/hiddenChannelsEffect/#Visualization","page":"Effect of hidden channels","title":"Visualization","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"generated/references/hiddenChannelsEffect/","page":"Effect of hidden channels","title":"Effect of hidden channels","text":"Now, we try to visualize the results. Here, we also highlight the advantages and drawbacks of increasing the latent space size","category":"page"},{"location":"generated/references/hiddenChannelsEffect/#Mean-of-input-data","page":"Effect of hidden channels","title":"Mean of input data","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"generated/references/hiddenChannelsEffect/","page":"Effect of hidden channels","title":"Effect of hidden channels","text":"series(mean(data, dims=3)[:, :, 1]; solid_color=:black)","category":"page"},{"location":"generated/references/hiddenChannelsEffect/#Mean-Predictions-for-Different-Hidden-Channels","page":"Effect of hidden channels","title":"Mean Predictions for Different Hidden Channels","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"generated/references/hiddenChannelsEffect/#10-hidden-channels","page":"Effect of hidden channels","title":"10 hidden channels","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"generated/references/hiddenChannelsEffect/","page":"Effect of hidden channels","title":"Effect of hidden channels","text":"series(mean(y_pred[1, :, :, :], dims=3)[:, :, 1]'; solid_color=:black)","category":"page"},{"location":"generated/references/hiddenChannelsEffect/#25-hidden-channels","page":"Effect of hidden channels","title":"25 hidden channels","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"generated/references/hiddenChannelsEffect/","page":"Effect of hidden channels","title":"Effect of hidden channels","text":"series(mean(y_pred[2, :, :, :], dims=3)[:, :, 1]'; solid_color=:black)","category":"page"},{"location":"generated/references/hiddenChannelsEffect/#50-hidden-channels","page":"Effect of hidden channels","title":"50 hidden channels","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"generated/references/hiddenChannelsEffect/","page":"Effect of hidden channels","title":"Effect of hidden channels","text":"series(mean(y_pred[3, :, :, :], dims=3)[:, :, 1]'; solid_color=:black)","category":"page"},{"location":"generated/references/hiddenChannelsEffect/#75-hidden-channels","page":"Effect of hidden channels","title":"75 hidden channels","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"generated/references/hiddenChannelsEffect/","page":"Effect of hidden channels","title":"Effect of hidden channels","text":"series(mean(y_pred[4, :, :, :], dims=3)[:, :, 1]'; solid_color=:black)","category":"page"},{"location":"generated/references/hiddenChannelsEffect/#100-hidden-channels","page":"Effect of hidden channels","title":"100 hidden channels","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"generated/references/hiddenChannelsEffect/","page":"Effect of hidden channels","title":"Effect of hidden channels","text":"series(mean(y_pred[5, :, :, :], dims=3)[:, :, 1]'; solid_color=:black)","category":"page"},{"location":"generated/references/hiddenChannelsEffect/#Loss-vs-Epoch-Plot","page":"Effect of hidden channels","title":"Loss vs Epoch Plot","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"generated/references/hiddenChannelsEffect/","page":"Effect of hidden channels","title":"Effect of hidden channels","text":"flattened_data_lossmse = []\nline_color_lossmse = [:orange, :brown, :red, :black, :blue]\nlabels_lossmse = [\"Hidden Channel 10\", \"Hidden Channel 25\", \"Hidden Channel 50\", \"Hidden Channel 75\", \"Hidden Channel 100\"]\nfor k in 1:5\n    push!(flattened_data_lossmse, [x[1] for x in lossepochdata[k]])\nend\nfig_lossmse = Figure()\nax_lossmse = Axis(fig_lossmse[1, 1], title=\"Loss vs Epoch\", xlabel=\"Epoch\", ylabel=\"Loss\")\nfor (i, data_lossmse) in enumerate(flattened_data_lossmse)\n    lines!(ax_lossmse, 1:length(data_lossmse), data_lossmse, label=labels_lossmse[i], color=line_color_lossmse[i])\nend\naxislegend(ax_lossmse)\nfig_lossmse","category":"page"},{"location":"generated/references/hiddenChannelsEffect/","page":"Effect of hidden channels","title":"Effect of hidden channels","text":"","category":"page"},{"location":"generated/references/hiddenChannelsEffect/#Conclusion","page":"Effect of hidden channels","title":"Conclusion","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"generated/references/hiddenChannelsEffect/","page":"Effect of hidden channels","title":"Effect of hidden channels","text":"This notebook demonstrates data simulation, model training, and visualization of deep recurrent encoding with different hidden channel sizes. The results provide insights into the effectiveness of different hidden channel configurations in predicting time-series data.","category":"page"}]
